  ('A-saturn_B-uranus_trine', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"saturn","planetB":"uranus","aspect":"trine"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-saturn_B-uranus_trine', 'synastry-saturn-trine-uranus', 'relationship-saturn-trine-uranus']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-saturn_B-uranus_trine.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-saturn_B-uranus_trine","importedStatus":"DRAFT"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Saturn Trine Uranus', null, 'A''s structure meets B''s need for freedom. The trine lets them flow together easily.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Translation","body":"A''s structure meets B''s need for freedom. The trine lets them flow together easily."}],"synastryKb":{"id":"A-saturn_B-uranus_trine","kind":"interaspect","plainTranslation":"A''s structure meets B''s need for freedom. The trine lets them flow together easily.","summaryDeep":null,"tension":null,"advice":null,"policy":"Governed by synastry-policy. Descriptive, not deterministic; intensity is never treated as obligation, safety concerns override astrology.","note":"Imported from project-authored synastry matrix material. Source file was not copied into the repository.","status":"DRAFT","authoringStatus":null,"weight":null}}'::jsonb, 'synastry_aspect', 'Seeded from authored synastry KB. summaryShort is preserved as NULL when absent or null.', now()),
  ('A-saturn_B-venus_conjunction', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"saturn","planetB":"venus","aspect":"conjunction"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-saturn_B-venus_conjunction', 'synastry-saturn-conjunction-venus', 'relationship-saturn-conjunction-venus']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-saturn_B-venus_conjunction.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-saturn_B-venus_conjunction","importedStatus":"DRAFT"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Saturn Conjunction Venus', null, 'This person can make love feel like something that has to be earned. Their reserved or cautious way of expressing affection may leave you wondering where you stand, even when they care deeply. Around them, old insecurities about being wanted or valued can surface, and you may find yourself putting extra pressure on yourself to get things right. Instead of relaxing into the connection, you may start trying to prove that you are worthy of the love and reassurance you want.

When this connection is functioning well, it can create a meaningful sense of commitment and emotional security. This person may not be naturally expressive or demonstrative with affection, but they often communicate care through reliability, responsibility, and a willingness to stay present when difficulties arise. Their approach to love may be thoughtful and serious, with a focus on creating something stable and lasting.

For many people, that can feel grounding. In a healthy Venus-Saturn dynamic, love is experienced not only through feelings, but through consistent actions. This connection can help you recognize that care is expressed in different ways. Sometimes it is found in words and affection, and sometimes it is reflected in dependability, effort, and follow-through.

At the same time, it is important to notice the difference between healthy emotional steadiness and emotional unavailability. They can look similar on the surface, but they have very different effects on a relationship. If this person has difficulty expressing affection or vulnerability, you may gradually begin lowering your expectations for emotional connection while convincing yourself that loyalty alone should meet your needs. It can be helpful to pay close attention to how you feel about yourself within this relationship. Do you feel accepted and valued, or do you often feel as though you need to prove your worth?

Consistency matters. Commitment matters. But someone staying is not the same thing as someone loving you in a way that meets your emotional needs. If you find yourself regularly longing for affection, attention, or reassurance, it may be worth asking whether loyalty alone is enough to sustain the relationship.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Deep meaning","body":"This person can make love feel like something that has to be earned. Their reserved or cautious way of expressing affection may leave you wondering where you stand, even when they care deeply. Around them, old insecurities about being wanted or valued can surface, and you may find yourself putting extra pressure on yourself to get things right. Instead of relaxing into the connection, you may start trying to prove that you are worthy of the love and reassurance you want.\n\nWhen this connection is functioning well, it can create a meaningful sense of commitment and emotional security. This person may not be naturally expressive or demonstrative with affection, but they often communicate care through reliability, responsibility, and a willingness to stay present when difficulties arise. Their approach to love may be thoughtful and serious, with a focus on creating something stable and lasting.\n\nFor many people, that can feel grounding. In a healthy Venus-Saturn dynamic, love is experienced not only through feelings, but through consistent actions. This connection can help you recognize that care is expressed in different ways. Sometimes it is found in words and affection, and sometimes it is reflected in dependability, effort, and follow-through.\n\nAt the same time, it is important to notice the difference between healthy emotional steadiness and emotional unavailability. They can look similar on the surface, but they have very different effects on a relationship. If this person has difficulty expressing affection or vulnerability, you may gradually begin lowering your expectations for emotional connection while convincing yourself that loyalty alone should meet your needs. It can be helpful to pay close attention to how you feel about yourself within this relationship. Do you feel accepted and valued, or do you often feel as though you need to prove your worth?\n\nConsistency matters. Commitment matters. But someone staying is not the same thing as someone loving you in a way that meets your emotional needs. If you find yourself regularly longing for affection, attention, or reassurance, it may be worth asking whether loyalty alone is enough to sustain the relationship."},{"heading":"Tension","body":"At the same time, it is important to notice the difference between healthy emotional steadiness and emotional unavailability. They can look similar on the surface, but they have very different effects on a relationship. If this person has difficulty expressing affection or vulnerability, you may gradually begin lowering your expectations for emotional connection while convincing yourself that loyalty alone should meet your needs. It can be helpful to pay close attention to how you feel about yourself within this relationship. Do you feel accepted and valued, or do you often feel as though you need to prove your worth?"},{"heading":"Advice","body":"Consistency matters. Commitment matters. But someone staying is not the same thing as someone loving you in a way that meets your emotional needs. If you find yourself regularly longing for affection, attention, or reassurance, it may be worth asking whether loyalty alone is enough to sustain the relationship."}],"synastryKb":{"id":"A-saturn_B-venus_conjunction","kind":"interaspect","plainTranslation":"The Saturn person makes the Venus person''s love feel serious, committed, and consequential. It can be loyal and long-lasting, but it may also feel cool, conditional, or hard to relax inside. Affection has to be shown, not assumed.","summaryDeep":"This person can make love feel like something that has to be earned. Their reserved or cautious way of expressing affection may leave you wondering where you stand, even when they care deeply. Around them, old insecurities about being wanted or valued can surface, and you may find yourself putting extra pressure on yourself to get things right. Instead of relaxing into the connection, you may start trying to prove that you are worthy of the love and reassurance you want.\n\nWhen this connection is functioning well, it can create a meaningful sense of commitment and emotional security. This person may not be naturally expressive or demonstrative with affection, but they often communicate care through reliability, responsibility, and a willingness to stay present when difficulties arise. Their approach to love may be thoughtful and serious, with a focus on creating something stable and lasting.\n\nFor many people, that can feel grounding. In a healthy Venus-Saturn dynamic, love is experienced not only through feelings, but through consistent actions. This connection can help you recognize that care is expressed in different ways. Sometimes it is found in words and affection, and sometimes it is reflected in dependability, effort, and follow-through.\n\nAt the same time, it is important to notice the difference between healthy emotional steadiness and emotional unavailability. They can look similar on the surface, but they have very different effects on a relationship. If this person has difficulty expressing affection or vulnerability, you may gradually begin lowering your expectations for emotional connection while convincing yourself that loyalty alone should meet your needs. It can be helpful to pay close attention to how you feel about yourself within this relationship. Do you feel accepted and valued, or do you often feel as though you need to prove your worth?\n\nConsistency matters. Commitment matters. But someone staying is not the same thing as someone loving you in a way that meets your emotional needs. If you find yourself regularly longing for affection, attention, or reassurance, it may be worth asking whether loyalty alone is enough to sustain the relationship.","tension":"At the same time, it is important to notice the difference between healthy emotional steadiness and emotional unavailability. They can look similar on the surface, but they have very different effects on a relationship. If this person has difficulty expressing affection or vulnerability, you may gradually begin lowering your expectations for emotional connection while convincing yourself that loyalty alone should meet your needs. It can be helpful to pay close attention to how you feel about yourself within this relationship. Do you feel accepted and valued, or do you often feel as though you need to prove your worth?","advice":"Consistency matters. Commitment matters. But someone staying is not the same thing as someone loving you in a way that meets your emotional needs. If you find yourself regularly longing for affection, attention, or reassurance, it may be worth asking whether loyalty alone is enough to sustain the relationship.","policy":"Governed by synastry-policy. Descriptive, not deterministic; intensity is never treated as obligation, safety concerns override astrology.","note":"Synced from authored synastry bundle synastry-authored__19_ (2).json.","status":"DRAFT","authoringStatus":"draft","weight":4}}'::jsonb, 'synastry_aspect', 'Seeded from authored synastry KB. summaryShort is preserved as NULL when absent or null.', now()),
  ('A-saturn_B-venus_opposition', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"saturn","planetB":"venus","aspect":"opposition"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-saturn_B-venus_opposition', 'synastry-saturn-opposition-venus', 'relationship-saturn-opposition-venus']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-saturn_B-venus_opposition.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-saturn_B-venus_opposition","importedStatus":"DRAFT"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Saturn Opposition Venus', null, 'This connection can bring up painful questions about love, worth, and emotional safety. You may find yourself longing for closeness while also feeling unsure whether it is truly available to you. There can be a sense of reaching for one another but missing each other emotionally, as if the care is there yet difficult to feel. One person may seek reassurance and connection, while the other responds with caution, distance, or a need to protect themselves. Over time, this can create frustration, loneliness, or self-doubt if those needs are not talked about openly and compassionately.

When this connection is functioning well, both people have an opportunity to grow. They may help you understand the importance of consistency, reliability, and following through on what you promise. You may help them recognize that love is not only about commitment, but also about emotional presence and making care visible. Together, there is potential to create a healthier balance between responsibility and emotional connection.

The difficulty often arises when the relationship starts orbiting a familiar wound. One person moves toward connection, hoping to feel secure. The other moves away, hoping to feel safe. Without awareness, this dynamic can become its own gravity, pulling both people into roles that leave them feeling unseen, misunderstood, and disconnected from the care that may still be present between them.

This connection asks for honesty about what love actually feels like in practice. Not just what is promised. Not just what is endured. What is actually received. The invitation here is to notice where loyalty has been mistaken for intimacy, or where commitment has been expected to carry the weight of unspoken needs.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Deep meaning","body":"This connection can bring up painful questions about love, worth, and emotional safety. You may find yourself longing for closeness while also feeling unsure whether it is truly available to you. There can be a sense of reaching for one another but missing each other emotionally, as if the care is there yet difficult to feel. One person may seek reassurance and connection, while the other responds with caution, distance, or a need to protect themselves. Over time, this can create frustration, loneliness, or self-doubt if those needs are not talked about openly and compassionately.\n\nWhen this connection is functioning well, both people have an opportunity to grow. They may help you understand the importance of consistency, reliability, and following through on what you promise. You may help them recognize that love is not only about commitment, but also about emotional presence and making care visible. Together, there is potential to create a healthier balance between responsibility and emotional connection.\n\nThe difficulty often arises when the relationship starts orbiting a familiar wound. One person moves toward connection, hoping to feel secure. The other moves away, hoping to feel safe. Without awareness, this dynamic can become its own gravity, pulling both people into roles that leave them feeling unseen, misunderstood, and disconnected from the care that may still be present between them.\n\nThis connection asks for honesty about what love actually feels like in practice. Not just what is promised. Not just what is endured. What is actually received. The invitation here is to notice where loyal