  ('A-saturn_B-venus_square', 'synastry', 'in_depth', 'LIVE', 'synastry-aspect', null::date, '{"kind":"interaspect","planetA":"saturn","planetB":"venus","aspect":"square"}'::jsonb, ARRAY['A-saturn_B-venus_square', 'synastry-saturn-square-venus', 'relationship-saturn-square-venus']::text[], '{"contentType":"synastry-kb-seed","source":"packages/astro-knowledge","sourcePath":"packages/astro-knowledge/data/synastry/aspects/A-saturn_B-venus_square.json","collection":"synastry/aspects","kbId":"A-saturn_B-venus_square","importedStatus":"DRAFT"}'::jsonb, 'synastry-kb-v1', null, 'Saturn Square Venus', null, 'This connection has a way of turning relationship tension into a question about your worth. When affection feels limited, inconsistent, or difficult to access, you may start focusing on what you need to do differently to receive more of it. The danger is that their restraint can begin to feel personal, as though the distance between you says something about your value. In reality, what you are experiencing may have far more to do with their fears, defenses, or way of relating than with anything lacking in you.

This aspect asks you to get honest about what love actually feels like. Are you being cared for, or are you constantly trying to earn approval? Are your needs being respected, or are you pushing them aside to keep the relationship working?

The challenge is falling into self-abandonment. You may keep adjusting, waiting, and trying harder, believing that if you are patient enough, easy enough, or understanding enough, the affection you want will finally appear. Flexibility is healthy. Losing yourself to maintain a connection is not. If one person keeps withholding, criticizing, or avoiding vulnerability while the other keeps overextending themselves to compensate, the pattern can become painful and difficult to sustain. But if both people are willing to acknowledge the problem, communicate honestly, and take responsibility for their own insecurities, this aspect can become a turning point rather than a reason for separation.

If someone struggles to give affection, reassurance, or emotional presence, that tells you something about them. It does not automatically tell you something about your value. Too many people stay stuck here, working harder and harder to earn what should be given freely in a healthy relationship. Take the insight this connection offers.', '{"sections":[{"heading":"Deep meaning","body":"This connection has a way of turning relationship tension into a question about your worth. When affection feels limited, inconsistent, or difficult to access, you may start focusing on what you need to do differently to receive more of it. The danger is that their restraint can begin to feel personal, as though the distance between you says something about your value. In reality, what you are experiencing may have far more to do with their fears, defenses, or way of relating than with anything lacking in you.\n\nThis aspect asks you to get honest about what love actually feels like. Are you being cared for, or are you constantly trying to earn approval? Are your needs being respected, or are you pushing them aside to keep the relationship working?\n\nThe challenge is falling into self-abandonment. You may keep adjusting, waiting, and trying harder, believing that if you are patient enough, easy enough, or understanding enough, the affection you want will finally appear. Flexibility is healthy. Losing yourself to maintain a connection is not. If one person keeps withholding, criticizing, or avoiding vulnerability while the other keeps overextending themselves to compensate, the pattern can become painful and difficult to sustain. But if both people are willing to acknowledge the problem, communicate honestly, and take responsibility for their own insecurities, this aspect can become a turning point rather than a reason for separation.\n\nIf someone struggles to give affection, reassurance, or emotional presence, that tells you something about them. It does not automatically tell you s